<title>GERMANY: Schaeuble-non-wage labour costs could be cut.</title>
<headline>Schaeuble-non-wage labour costs could be cut.</headline>
<dateline>BERLIN 1997-04-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Wolfgang Schaeuble, parliamentary leader of German Chancellor Helmut Kohl's Christian Democrats, said on Wednesday that a reduction in non-wage labour costs could possibly be financed through other taxes.</p>
<p>Asked if such a refinancing of social security contributions that are currently charged as part of wages, Schaeuble said &quot;to a certain extent yes&quot;.</p>
<p>Just after the close of a two-day union-organised jobs conference, Schaeuble said that he was ready to discuss a refinancing of such contributions toward other areas such as value-added tax and energy taxes.</p>
<p>Talks between the government and the opposition Social Democrats on a major tax reform are due to recommence next Tuesday.</p>
<p>--Mark John, Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
